---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Why this site exists, and what the name means: I can chan — 我参禅, 我能禅, and finally no self, no practice, no Chan."
  path="/about/"
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Why this site exists.</h1>
      <p>
        Because the West met the branch and mistook it for the root. Because a tradition that asks you to
        think was sold as a technique to relax.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="domain-block">
    <div class="container">
      <img class="domain-glyph" src="/assets/chan-logo.png" alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
      <div class="reading" style="text-align:center">
        <span class="eyebrow">The name</span>
        <h2 class="serif" style="font-size:clamp(30px,4vw,42px);margin:14px 0 18px">I can chan.</h2>
        <p class="body-copy" style="font-size:17px">
          The domain is a sentence, not a brand. Read it three ways and you have the whole site.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="domain-rows">
        <div class="domain-row">
          <span class="zh-line">我参禅</span>
          <span class="en-line">I take part in Chan.</span>
          <p>
            The plain reading. Not “I believe in Chan” — Chan is not a thing to believe. You sit, you
            watch, you check. Participation, then evidence.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="domain-row">
          <span class="zh-line">我能禅</span>
          <span class="en-line">I am able to Chan.</span>
          <p>
            The claim underneath it. This is a capacity, not a membership — not reserved for monks, not
            for the spiritually gifted. Anyone willing to look at their own mind qualifies.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="domain-row">
          <span class="zh-line">禅即日用</span>
          <span class="en-line">Chan is what the day is made of.</span>
          <p>
            The consequence. If it only works on a cushion, it does not work. Washing up, answering
            email, sitting with someone who is grieving — same practice, different posture.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="domain-coda">
        <p>到最后，无我、无参、也无禅。</p>
        <p>
          Which is where the sentence runs out. The point was never to become good at Chan. It was to
          let the whole thing dissolve into ordinary life until there is no one doing it, no practice to
          perform, and nothing left to call Chan. No self, no practising, no Chan — just the day, done
          properly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body container">
    <div class="reading">
      <p class="lead-serif">
        Chan is the Chinese source of Zen. Not a rival to it — its parent.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        When Chan reached Japan in the twelfth century it became Zen: refined, formalised, aestheticised.
        The tea ceremony, the rock garden, the bowing — Japanese inventions, beautiful and precise. But they
        are the branch. The root stayed in China, in a tradition that was blunter, less performative, and
        far more interested in seeing than in sounding wise.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        What the English-speaking world mostly received was the branch, wrapped in mystique: incense, robes,
        enigmatic riddles. Meditation became the one part that could be de-religionised and scanned in a lab.
        Everything else was quietly left behind.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        This site puts the rest back. The three trainings, the history, the arguments, the text itself —
        and the practice, which in Chan happens standing, walking, eating and arguing, not only on a cushion.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container divider-top">
    <div class="reading">
      <span class="eyebrow">About the author</span>
      <h2 style="font-size:34px;margin:14px 0 24px">Hu</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">
        I grew up in China with no religion, and a lasting fondness for Buddhist thought. I read the
        classics; I do not go to temples to bow. Modern education made me a rational critic first.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        Of all the traditions I have read, Buddhism is the one I respect most — because it does not demand
        belief. It asks you to think. That fits a modern, scientifically trained mind better than either
        fervent scientism or fervent faith.
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        The problem is that the West mostly received the practice and missed the thought. So this site is an
        attempt to hand over the deeper part: Chan as a way of seeing, not a way of calming down.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="serif" style="font-style:italic;font-size:24px;line-height:1.6;border-left:2px solid var(--gold);padding-left:24px;margin:40px 0;color:var(--forest)">
        “禅本来是一种精神，很随意，没有固定的招数。禅像冲浪：自由，挑战，一种说不清的人生艺术。”
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container divider-top">
    <div class="reading">
      <span class="eyebrow">What this is not</span>
      <ul class="pillar-list" style="margin-top:20px">
        <li>Not a religion. Nothing here asks you to believe.</li>
        <li>Not a meditation course. Practice is one third of the training, not the whole of it.</li>
        <li>Not a translation of Japanese Zen. Where the two differ, we say so.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
